en theory and practice? What are the conceptual and geographical reaches of the term \"Asian American,\" particularly as it pertains to Asian North American and Pacific Islander studies, and their critiques of settler colonialism and imperialism? What coalitions between Asian Americans and other marginalized groups have been and continue to be forged? The course will examine these questions by thinking about issues of gender, sexuality, nationality, ability and class.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2692, "crseId": 357919, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "2620", "titleShort": "Intro to Asian Am Lit", "titleLong": "Introduction to Asian American Literature", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 17219, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:40AM", "timeEnd": "12:55PM", "startDt": "01/24/2018", "endDt": "05/09/2018", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ssw6", "firstName": "Sunn", "middleName": "Shelley", "lastName": "Wong"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Sibley Hall 115", "bldgDescr": "Sibley Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "SBL115", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/24/2018", "endDt": "05/09/2018", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "2620", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "2620", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/24/2018", "sessionEndDt": "05/09/2018", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This course will introduce both a variety of writings by Asian North American authors and some critical issues concerning the production and reception of Asian American texts. Working primarily with novels, we will be asking questions about the relation between literary forms and the socio-historical context within which they take on their meanings, and about the historical formation of Asian American identities.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(LA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2692, "crseId": 369423, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "3015", "titleShort": "Cyborg Writing: Diasporic Lit", "titleLong": "Cyborg Writing: Diasporic Literary Media", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 18102, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "01/24/2018", "endDt": "05/09/2018", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 3, "netid": "dw578", "firstName": "Danielle", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Wong"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall G20", "bldgDescr": "Goldwin Smith Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "GSHG20", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/24/2018", "endDt": "05/09/2018", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "3915", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "3015", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/24/2018", "sessionEndDt": "05/09/2018", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This course examines the relationships between diasporic/transnational experiences and emerging forms, practices and technologies of literary production. We will analyze literary media, such as interactive graphic novels, text-based web art, multimedia poetry, unconventional novels and blogs in order to study Asian racial formation and cultural production. In particular, we will discuss the political act of writing and reading by considering feminist \"cyborg writing\"\u2014a term coined by Donna Haraway\u2014and thinking about gender, race, sexuality, labor and migration.\u00a0", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2692, "crseId": 350721, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "3030", "titleShort": "Asians in the Americas", "titleLong": "Asians in the Americas: A Comparative Perspective", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 9103, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:55PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "01/24/2018", "endDt": "05/09/2018", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "vpm1", "firstName": "Viranjini", "middleName": "P.", "lastName": "Munasinghe"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "McGraw Hall B65", "bldgDescr": "McGraw Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "MCGB65", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/24/2018", "endDt": "05/09/2018", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "3703", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "ANTHR", "catalogNbr": "3703", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "ANTHR", "catalogNbr": "6703", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/24/2018", "sessionEndDt": "05/09/2018", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "The common perception of ethnicity is that it is a \"natural\" and an inevitable consequence of cultural difference. \"Asians\" overseas, in particular, have won repute as a people who cling tenaciously to their culture and refuse to assimilate into their host societies and cultures. But, who are the \"Asians?\" On what basis can we label \"Asians\" an ethnic group? Although there is a significant Asian presence in the Caribbean, the category \"Asian\" itself does not exist in the Caribbean. What does this say about the nature of categories that label and demarcate groups of people on the basis of alleged cultural and phenotypical characteristics? This course will examine the dynamics behind group identity, namely ethnicity, by comparing and contrasting the multicultural experience of Asian populations in the Caribbean and the United States. Ethnographic case studies will focus on the East Indian and Chinese experiences in the Caribbean and the Chinese, Korean, Japanese, Filipino, and Indian experiences in the United States.", "catalogBreadth": "(HB)", "catalogDistr": "(CA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2692, "crseId": 351007, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "4950", "titleShort": "Independent Study", "titleLong": "Independent Study", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": 